{"name":"Adirondack","hex":"#b0b9c1","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Adirondack","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A member of an Algonquin people that lived along the Ottawa River in Ontario and Quebec."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The Adirondacks ."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Adirondack"}],"entries":[{"title":"Adirondack Mountains","blocks":[{"text":"The Adirondack Mountains are a massif of mountains in Northeastern New York State which form a circular dome approximately 160 miles (260 km) wide and covering about 5,000 square miles (13,000 km2). The region contains more than 100 peaks, including Mount Marcy, which is the highest point in New York at 5,344 feet (1,629 m). The Adirondack High Peaks, a traditional list of 46 peaks over 4,000 feet (1,200 m), are popular hiking destinations. There are over 200 named lakes with the number of smaller lakes, ponds, and other bodies of water reaching over 3,000, amidst the thousands of square miles of Adirondack taiga boreal forest. Among the named lakes around the mountains are Lake George, Lake Placid, and Lake Tear of the Clouds. The region has over 1,200 miles (1,900 km) of rivers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg/330px-Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adirondack_Mountains"},{"title":"Adirondack Park","blocks":[{"text":"The Adirondack Park is the designation for a large area of northeastern New York centered on the Adirondack Mountains. Like Catskill Park to the south, the area is unusual in the United States because, while the entire area is considered \"parkland\", about 52 percent of the land within the boundary consists of privately owned inholdings. The remaining 48 percent is publicly owned by the state as part of the Forest Preserve. Use of public and private lands in the park is regulated by the Adirondack Park Agency."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Long_Pond_-_St_Regis.jpg/330px-Long_Pond_-_St_Regis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adirondack_Park"},{"title":"Adirondack (train)","blocks":[{"text":"The Adirondack is a daily intercity passenger train operated by Amtrak between New York City and Montreal. The scenic route follows the Empire Corridor through the Hudson Valley with major stops in Yonkers, Poughkeepsie, Albany–Rensselaer, and Schenectady. North of Saratoga Springs the route runs between the Adirondack Mountains and Lake Champlain until crossing the Canada–United States border at Rouses Point. Trains take approximately 11 hours to travel the 381-mile (613 km) route."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Saratoga_Springs_Rail_Station_%E2%80%93_Adirondack_Train_02.jpg/330px-Saratoga_Springs_Rail_Station_%E2%80%93_Adirondack_Train_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adirondack_(train)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Diopside_marble_%28GeoDIL_number_-_694%29.jpg/500px-Diopside_marble_%28GeoDIL_number_-_694%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Diopside_marble_%28GeoDIL_number_-_694%29.jpg/1280px-Diopside_marble_%28GeoDIL_number_-_694%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Diopside_marble_%28GeoDIL_number_-_694%29.jpg/500px-Diopside_marble_%28GeoDIL_number_-_694%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Green diopside and gray/white calcite in marble from the Adirondack Mountains","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiopside_marble_(GeoDIL_number_-_694).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Whiteface_Mountain_from_Lake_Placid_Airport.JPG/500px-Whiteface_Mountain_from_Lake_Placid_Airport.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Whiteface_Mountain_from_Lake_Placid_Airport.JPG/1280px-Whiteface_Mountain_from_Lake_Placid_Airport.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Whiteface_Mountain_from_Lake_Placid_Airport.JPG/500px-Whiteface_Mountain_from_Lake_Placid_Airport.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Whiteface Mountain (1,483 m or 4,867 ft) is the fifth-highest mountain in New York, and one of the High Peaks of the Adirondack Mountains.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWhiteface_Mountain_from_Lake_Placid_Airport.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/SpottedNorthern.png/500px-SpottedNorthern.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/SpottedNorthern.png/500px-SpottedNorthern.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A spotted turtle at the Wild Center","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpottedNorthern.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Adirondacks_in_May_2008.jpg/500px-Adirondacks_in_May_2008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Adirondacks_in_May_2008.jpg/960px-Adirondacks_in_May_2008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Adirondacks_in_May_2008.jp